Keep member elements in one parent chain

Reject membership insertion and equality across distinct parent models
so the public API cannot create ill-typed member elements. Remember
allocation parents separately from relation indices so checks respect
equalities even before closure, and validate nested parents before
allocating an element.

Do not propagate membership: every image receives its parent chain at
allocation, and propagation can otherwise expose extra parents before
functionality equalities settle. Include pending morphism endpoints in
the temporary traversal graph without adding membership facts.

Cover rejection for plain, model, morphism and enum members, valid
parent merges, and ownership throughout incremental morphism closure.
Replace fixtures that depended on multiple memberships with valid cases.
Verify that rules assigning multiple parents remain compile errors.
